<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  **Cleartext HTTP, deliberately and narrowly.**

  Android has blocked plain HTTP by default since API 28, and it is right to.
  But `chaos-serve` speaks HTTP on a LAN address: there is no certificate for
  `192.168.1.10` and there is no authority that would issue one. Refusing
  cleartext would mean the app could not talk to the only thing it exists to
  talk to.

  So cleartext is permitted, and the API key is what actually guards the
  endpoint -- which is why `chaos-serve` refuses to bind a non-loopback address
  without one. Traffic on the local Wi-Fi is readable by anyone already on that
  Wi-Fi; anyone sending something they would not say on that network should not
  be sending it over this.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
